-a882-7330c0dcbd51"] [17592186198280 :knowledge-point/questions "question-b5cdaff3-81ba-58ae-a27f-1dea94cd990d"] ["question-29e9adcf-4a5b-51ac-835b-f4a444c16f67" :question/id #uuid "29e9adcf-4a5b-51ac-835b-f4a444c16f67"] ["question-29e9adcf-4a5b-51ac-835b-f4a444c16f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-175346"] ["question-29e9adcf-4a5b-51ac-835b-f4a444c16f67" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is a negative number.\n\nII. $q$ is a multiple of $3.$\n\nIII. $p$ is an irrational number."] ["question-29e9adcf-4a5b-51ac-835b-f4a444c16f67" :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $q$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $p$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] ["question-29e9adcf-4a5b-51ac-835b-f4a444c16f67" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-32567726-3501-5d0d-940f-48d231eb6ef4" :answer-field/id #uuid "32567726-3501-5d0d-940f-48d231eb6ef4"] ["answer-e51cc954-0758-54e1-9a16-24656e0a29ea" :answer/id #uuid "e51cc954-0758-54e1-9a16-24656e0a29ea"] ["answer-e51cc954-0758-54e1-9a16-24656e0a29ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e51cc954-0758-54e1-9a16-24656e0a29ea" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-73240a1b-211b-5c00-9